# CurseForge release tags (GearQuest Forever)

Full workflow: [RELEASE.md](../../RELEASE.md).

This is a **separate** CurseForge project from TBC Anniversary GearQuest. Never copy `X-Curse-Project-ID` or GitHub `CF_API_KEY` from that repo.

## The right way

Publishing is **GitHub Actions**, not the CurseForge webhook (webhook must stay **inactive**):

1. User must have **`CF_API_KEY`** set in **this** repo's Secrets (CurseForge API token).
2. Push a version tag (`v0.2.1-beta`) → `.github/workflows/release.yml` runs.
3. Workflow uses `.pkgmeta` → `BigWigsMods/packager@v2` uploads to CurseForge.
4. Verify **GitHub Actions → Release** succeeds; check CurseForge **Files**.

Pushing to `main` alone is **not** a release. Do **not** delete/re-push tags to retry — bump patch version instead.

## Do not tag unless asked

Never create or push a CurseForge release tag unless the user **explicitly** requests publishing (e.g. "release to CurseForge", "push v0.1.0 to CurseForge").

## When the user asks for CurseForge

1. Bump `GearQuest/GearQuestForever.toc` `## Version:` and `GearQuest/Core.lua` `GQ.VERSION` to `X.Y.Z`. `v1.0.0` is the first release-channel file. Use `X.Y.Z-beta` only when the user asks for a beta. File name on CurseForge is the tag (`v1.0.0`, or `vX.Y.Z-beta` for a beta). The log header must read **`GearQuest Forever v`** + that same version (`GearQuestWindowTitle` in `GearQuest/Log.lua` uses `GQ.VERSION`). Do not hardcode a separate header version; every release bump of `GQ.VERSION` updates the band.
2. Add `## vX.Y.Z` at the top of `CHANGELOG.md` and demote the previous `## ` heading to `###`. `scripts/upload-curseforge-forever.py` sends markdown from that first `## ` line through the end of the file (`changelogType` is `markdown`). Older versions stay underneath as `### v…` so there is only one level-2 heading. Do not split the file on the characters `## `; a `###` heading contains those characters and used to cut the upload off at the first subsection. Write the new section for players, in the detail of `v0.2.19-beta`: a short lead, then a subsection per change. Each subsection says what the player sees, which classes, specs, and levels, and the concrete windows or behavior. Name the sets, the pieces, and the levels. A one-line summary is not the changelog. Older versions stay in the same file, shorter than the newest section. Do not delete them.
3. Confirm `## X-Curse-Project-ID:` is the **Forever** project, not TBC GearQuest (`1669225`).
4. Commit and push to `main`.
5. `git tag v<version>` and `git push origin v<version>`. A tag that does not end in `-beta` uploads on the CurseForge **release** channel. A tag that ends in `-beta` uploads on the **beta** channel. `.github/workflows/release.yml` sets `CF_RELEASE_TYPE` from the tag name.
6. Confirm GitHub Actions **Release** job succeeded; remind user to check CurseForge **Files**.

Do **not** tag when the user only asked to commit, push, or update docs.
